The rewrite keeps what the plugin gave a user: the two tools jp_corp_search and jp_corp_profile
with their names, arguments, sections and answer fields; the gBizINFO REST API v2 they call, its
one header and its one host; the check digit and the folded names and addresses that decide what
is sent; the ordering of a name search (exact match first, registered before closed); the
attribution line the terms of use ask for; and the conservative manners (a ten-minute in-memory
cache, one request every half second, no retries, no pagination of its own). What changed is the
language and the runtime: Python with the Hermes tool contract became Common Lisp with
NLK:DEFINE-CONTRACT and NLE:DEFINE-CELL, the section of a Hermes profile's .env became the cell's
section in ~/.nodecode/config.jsonc (with GBIZINFO_API_TOKEN still read from the environment), and
the JSON answers the plugin returned became laid-out text with the same members. Left out: the
plugin's tests and its after-install checklist, which belonged to Hermes; the exact reproducing
of JSON member order (the text is sorted by member name); and the redirect refusal the Python
client wrote by hand, since the shared HTTP seam follows a redirect itself — the token still goes
to one host, api.info.gbiz.go.jp.
